{% extends "layout.njk" %}
{% import "macros.njk" as m %}
{% set title = "Studio site sections" %}
{% set slug = "patterns-studio" %}

{% block content %}
<h1>Studio site sections</h1>
<p class="lede">
  Sections from
  <a href="https://sparkstonepdx.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">sparkstonepdx.com</a>,
  rebuilt in the class build with the site's own structure and copy. Images are
  left out: where the site shows a photo, these show the same block the site
  uses for a post with no image. The rules behind them are on
  <a href="./composition.html">Composition</a>.
</p>

<section id="header">
  <h2>Header</h2>
  <p>
    The logo on the left, the section links and the booking link on the right.
    On the site the bar is sticky and blurs what scrolls under it; on a phone the
    links hide and a ghost menu button takes their place.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<header class="navbar">
  <div class="navbar-start">
    <a href="#" class="semantic" aria-label="Home"><strong>Sparkstone</strong></a>
  </div>
  <nav class="navbar-end">
    <a href="#" class="link">Case Studies</a>
    <a href="#" class="link">Products</a>
    <a href="#" class="link">Blog</a>
    <a href="#" class="link">Open Source</a>
    <a href="#" class="link">Book a call</a>
  </nav>
</header>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-header", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Layout:</strong> the site pushes the links right with an empty <code>flex-grow</code> spacer. <code>.navbar-start</code> and <code>.navbar-end</code> do the same with auto margins, so the spacer goes.</li>
    <li><strong>Actions:</strong> "Book a call" is a plain link in the header, the same weight as its neighbours. The filled button is saved for the hero.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="hero">
  <h2>Hero</h2>
  <p>
    A heading, one paragraph, one button. The site sets the heading at
    <code>1.5rem</code> in its muted text colour, so it reads as a lead-in to the
    paragraph rather than a headline.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<section class="container prose" style="max-width: 80ch">
  <h1 class="text-secondary" style="font-size: 1.5rem">Custom software and websites for medical practices, law firms, and specialty contractors in Portland.</h1>
  <p>I'm Adam. I run Sparkstone, a one-person software studio. I build the systems your business actually runs on: intake, scheduling, referral portals, client dashboards, and the sites that feed them. I work on a monthly retainer, so the person who built your systems is the one keeping them running.</p>
  <a href="#" role="button" class="btn">Book a call</a>
</section>
<section class="container prose" style="max-width: 80ch">
  <p>Featured on <a href="#" class="link">Behind the Creative Curtain</a>, <a href="#" class="link">NarwhalPod</a>, and <a href="#" class="link">Creative Mornings</a></p>
</section>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-hero", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Layout:</strong> every section on the site is its own <code>.container</code> capped at <code>80ch</code>, the reading measure, so sections stack with nothing wrapping them.</li>
    <li><strong>Text:</strong> <code>.prose</code> sits on each container. It spaces the heading, paragraph and button, and gives each container a bottom margin to the next.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="services">
  <h2>Services</h2>
  <p>
    Four services as cards in a wrapping row, then a card that sends anyone
    still unsure to a call.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<section class="prose">
  <h2>Services</h2>
  <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 20rem), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Custom Applications</h3>
      <p>the internal tool or portal you can't buy off the shelf</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Project rescue</h3>
      <p>take over a build that stalled, or clean up after a vendor</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Fractional CTO</h3>
      <p>support for teams that need direction, architecture, or product leadership without full-time overhead.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Technical review</h3>
      <p>a paid second opinion on a vendor's proposal before you sign</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <article class="card" style="text-align: center; padding: 2rem">
    <p>Not sure which one fits? Let's talk it through</p>
    <a href="#" role="button" class="btn">Book a consultation</a>
  </article>
</section>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-services", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Layout:</strong> a grid of columns at least <code>20rem</code> wide, so the four cards sit two by two inside the <code>80ch</code> measure and stack on a phone. <code>min(100%, 20rem)</code> stops a column overflowing a screen narrower than <code>20rem</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface:</strong> the call to action is the same <code>.card</code> as the services, centred, with double padding. The site also lays a faint radial highlight over it, left out here.</li>
    <li><strong>Spacing:</strong> the grid is a <code>div</code>, which <code>.prose</code> does not space, so it carries its own <code>1rem</code> bottom margin, as on the site.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="testimonials">
  <h2>Testimonials</h2>
  <p>
    Quotes side by side, each attributed under its text.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<section class="prose">
  <h2>Testimonials</h2>
  <div class="flex flip">
    <blockquote style="flex: 1">
      <p>I am forever grateful to liveframe for giving us the opportunity to see our big day from the perspective of our loved ones.</p>
      &mdash; <i>Allie, A Liveframe User</i>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote style="flex: 1">
      <p>We are a tiny team with big dreams of making zines more accessible for everyone. Adam showed sincere interest in our project, listened to our ramblings, asked great questions, and helped us build a website beyond what we'd hoped for. He was extremely creative, organized, and responsive throughout the process, which made him a great collaborator.</p>
      &mdash; <i>Wendy Shih, Little Zine Revolution</i>
    </blockquote>
  </div>
</section>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-testimonials", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Text:</strong> the quotes are prose, not cards. <code>.prose</code> gives each <code>blockquote</code> its <code>--line</code> rule and padding, and spaces the paragraph inside it.</li>
    <li><strong>Layout:</strong> <code>.flex</code> aligns the row's items to the top by default, so a short quote beside a long one stays at the top rather than stretching its rule.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="post-list">
  <h2>Post list</h2>
  <p>
    Case studies, products and blog posts share one list: each entry is a link
    wrapping an image and its title and summary. A post with no image shows its
    title in a tinted block instead, which is what these use.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--padding)">
  <a href="#" class="semantic">
    <article class="flex flip">
      <div style="flex: 1 1 30%; aspect-ratio: 1.5; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; padding: var(--padding); border-radius: var(--border-radius); background: var(--surface-strong); color: var(--primary-text); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: bold">The Map I Never Meant to Build</div>
      <div class="prose" style="flex: 1 1 70%">
        <h3 class="text-secondary">The Map I Never Meant to Build</h3>
        <p>Sixty thousand views, three neighborhoods asking to borrow it, and a little map that got a bit better every spring until it turned into something anyone can run.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </a>
  <a href="#" class="semantic">
    <article class="flex flip">
      <div style="flex: 1 1 30%; aspect-ratio: 1.5; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; padding: var(--padding); border-radius: var(--border-radius); background: var(--surface-strong); color: var(--primary-text); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: bold">A Library That Fits in a Free Little Library</div>
      <div class="prose" style="flex: 1 1 70%">
        <h3 class="text-secondary">A Library That Fits in a Free Little Library</h3>
        <p>A zine catalog, a printing guide, and an events page, built from a pile of draft copy in about two weeks.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </a>
</div>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-post-list", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Actions:</strong> the whole entry is one <code>a.semantic</code>, which strips the link styling. The title is in the muted text colour, as on the site.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface:</strong> on the site each entry is an <code>article</code>, which the classless build turns into a card. In the class build the article has no class, so the entries sit on the page, as here.</li>
    <li><strong>Boundary:</strong> the site tints the no-image block with its accent colour. In v3 that is a <code>.retheme</code> with <code>--primary</code> set to the accent around the block, if you want it to differ from the title.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="link-previews">
  <h2>Link previews</h2>
  <p>
    The free tools page: each tool is an outside link, shown with an image, its
    name, a sentence, and the address it goes to. The images are placeholders.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<div class="prose">
  <h1>Free Tools</h1>
  <p>Some things I build for my own projects turn out to be useful on their own. These are free to use, need no account, and don't hold your data hostage.</p>
</div>
<a href="#" target="_blank" class="semantic" style="display: block; margin-bottom: 1rem">
  <article class="card" style="display: flex; padding: 0; overflow: hidden">
    <div style="flex: none; width: 8rem; height: 8rem; background: var(--surface-strong)" role="img" aria-label=""></div>
    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.25rem; min-width: 0; margin: 1rem">
      <strong>QR Code &amp; Table Tent Generator</strong>
      <p>Make a styled QR code for any link, then print foldable table tents for events, counters, or waiting rooms.</p>
      <small class="text-secondary" style="overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap">https://liveframe.app/promo/qr-table</small>
    </div>
  </article>
</a>
<a href="#" target="_blank" class="semantic" style="display: block; margin-bottom: 1rem">
  <article class="card" style="display: flex; padding: 0; overflow: hidden">
    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.25rem; min-width: 0; margin: 1rem">
      <strong>Background Remover</strong>
      <p>Remove the background from a photo right in your browser. Your images never leave your computer.</p>
      <small class="text-secondary" style="overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap">https://sparkstonepdx.github.io/bgremove/</small>
    </div>
  </article>
</a>
<a href="#" target="_blank" class="semantic" style="display: block; margin-bottom: 1rem">
  <article class="card" style="display: flex; padding: 0; overflow: hidden">
    <div style="flex: none; width: 8rem; height: 8rem; background: var(--surface-strong)" role="img" aria-label=""></div>
    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.25rem; min-width: 0; margin: 1rem">
      <strong>AnimLab</strong>
      <p>Pick out elements of a drawing and animate them. Runs from your browser's devtools, so it's best for the curious and technical.</p>
      <small class="text-secondary" style="overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap">https://github.com/sparkstonepdx/animlab</small>
    </div>
  </article>
</a>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-link-previews", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Actions:</strong> the anchor wraps the card and is set to <code>display: block</code>, because <code>.semantic</code> unsets it to inline.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface:</strong> the card drops its padding and clips its corners so the image runs to the edge; the text column puts the padding back as a margin. A tool with no image, like the background remover, simply has no image column.</li>
    <li><strong>Text:</strong> the address is <code>.text-secondary</code> so it reads as a footnote to the description, not as a second link.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="about">
  <h2>About block</h2>
  <p>
    A photo beside a name, a role, and one line, with a link to the full page.
    The photo is a placeholder here.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<section class="prose">
  <h2>About Sparkstone</h2>
  <div class="flex flip">
    <div style="width: 8rem; aspect-ratio: 1; flex-shrink: 0; border: 2px solid var(--line); border-radius: 1rem; background: var(--surface-strong)" role="img" aria-label="photo of Adam Sparks"></div>
    <div style="max-width: 25rem">
      <h3 style="margin: 0">Adam Sparks</h3>
      <i class="text-secondary" style="display: block">Founder</i>
      <p>Everyone should have the freedom to build software they’re proud of without sacrificing joy, balance, or humanity.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <a href="#" class="link">About Sparkstone</a>
</section>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-about", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Layout:</strong> <code>.flex .flip</code> puts the photo above the text on a phone. <code>flex-shrink: 0</code> stops the text squeezing the photo on a narrow screen.</li>
    <li><strong>Text:</strong> the role is <code>.text-secondary</code> and set to block so it sits on its own line under the name.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="newsletter">
  <h2>Newsletter</h2>
  <p>
    A heading, a sentence, one labelled field and a secondary button. It is a
    secondary button because the page's main action is booking a call.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<form class="prose">
  <h2>Newsletter</h2>
  <p>Subscribe to my newsletter to be the first to hear about what I do next</p>
  <label class="label">
    <p>Email</p>
    <input class="input" type="email" placeholder="Email" autocomplete="email" required style="max-width: 30rem" />
  </label>
  <button class="btn btn-secondary">Subscribe</button>
</form>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-newsletter", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Controls:</strong> the <code>.label</code> spaces its text and field, and the field's own bottom margin spaces the button, so the form needs no layout of its own.</li>
    <li><strong>States:</strong> when the address fails, set <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> on the input and add a <code>&lt;small&gt;</code> after it. The label text and field turn to the error colour together.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="closing-cta">
  <h2>Closing call to action</h2>
  <p>
    The end of every case study: a heading that names the reader's problem, one
    sentence, and the booking button.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<section class="prose">
  <h2>Need a site that does one job well?</h2>
  <p>If you've got the words and none of the time, that's the version of this work I do most often.</p>
  <a href="#" role="button" class="btn">Book a call</a>
</section>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-closing-cta", classless) }}
</section>

<section id="footer">
  <h2>Footer</h2>
  <p>
    A rule, then two groups: the logo, tagline and social links, and the full
    list of site links. The site uses icons for the social links; these use
    their names.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<footer>
  <hr class="divider" />
  <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 20rem), 1fr)); gap: 1rem">
    <section>
      <strong>Sparkstone</strong>
      <p>Where Technology Sparks Innovation</p>
      <div class="flex">
        <a href="#" class="link">YouTube</a>
        <a href="#" class="link">LinkedIn</a>
        <a href="#" class="link">Bluesky</a>
        <a href="#" class="link">GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <nav class="flex" style="flex-direction: column; align-items: end">
      <a href="#" class="link">Case Studies</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Products</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Blog</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Open Source</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Book a call</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Free Tools</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Mentions</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Alignment</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">About</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</footer>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-footer", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Surface:</strong> <code>.divider</code> draws the same <code>--divider</code> rule a <code>.card-border</code> uses, so the footer is separated without being boxed in.</li>
    <li><strong>Layout:</strong> two columns at least <code>20rem</code> wide. The links are <code>.flex</code> turned on its side and aligned to the end, so they hug the right edge. On a phone the site switches them to align at the start; that needs a media query of your own.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endblock %}
